Canonicalization

Canonicalization is a set of tools for bringing a system into a canonicalized state: a declaratively defined NixOS system, with an ephemeral root filesystem and explicitly preserved state; and a home directory managed as a Git repository with a whitelist `.gitignore`.

The home repository organizes projects in a Go-style path layout (for example, `github.com/owner/project`). Projects are Git submodules whose own repositories use whitelist `.gitignore` files, are Nix flakes, and follow a small, purpose-built subset of the https://github.com/numtide/blueprint directory layout. Packages contain a single source file. Conventional formatters and strict linters are applied.

`git canonicalization` creates and enforces these home and flake repository conventions.

Home repository:

```sh
cd "$HOME"
git init
printf '*\n!/.gitignore\n!/.gitmodules\n' > .gitignore # also add other required files/directories like .ssh
git add .gitignore
git commit -m 'Initialize home repository'
```

```sh
# Create the empty remote before starting this workflow.
cd "$HOME"
git submodule add --force git@github.com:owner/demo.git github.com/owner/demo
cd github.com/owner/demo
git canonicalization init .
git add flake.nix flake.lock .gitignore
git commit -m 'Initialize flake repository'
cd "$HOME"
git add .gitmodules github.com/owner/demo
git commit -m 'Add demo repository'
```

Flake repositories: `git canonicalization add <type> <name>` targets the selected repository, follows a restricted subset of the numtide/blueprint layout, and maintains its root `.gitignore` whitelist.
README: explains the repository's intent.
Test names: describe the expected behavior.
Code: implements the expected behavior. `nix fmt` runs conventional formatters and strict linters; `nix flake check` verifies the canonical structure and runs builds and tests.
